  "locus": "Theses in quibus exponitur Doctrina Protestantium circa distinctionem Gratiae in sufficientem atque efficacem, & concordiam libertatis humanae cum efficacia gratiae divinae, LXVII-LXXXVIII, end of locus (concluding comparisons: the Remonstrants and the Augsburg theologians agree in substance, and the Remonstrants' view is tolerable; the Remonstrants agree with Molina's followers; Dort agrees with the recent Thomists and the Jansenists, the dispute on 'sufficient grace' being verbal (the Nassau-Wetterau theologians at Dort); differences on the nature of efficacious grace are scholastic; a plea to drop arduous questions and preach what Christians agree on; appendix on today's Socinians (Philippians 2:13; Volkelius, De vera religione 5.18)). The Protestant locus ends with LXXXVIII on book 554, followed on book 555 by the title of the locus on whether and how far man can fulfil the Law by Christ's grace.",
